<body>

<div class="container">

    <h1>Linux Commands and Their Functions</h1>

    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>Introduction to Linux</h2>

        <p>
            Linux is an open-source operating system widely used in modern IT infrastructure.
            It is famous for its security, stability, speed, and flexibility.
            Linux powers servers, cloud platforms, mobile devices, supercomputers,
            networking systems, and enterprise applications across the world.
        </p>

        <p>
            Linux was created by <span class="highlight">Linus Torvalds</span> in 1991.
            Because Linux is open-source, developers can modify and distribute it freely.
        </p>

        <h3>Why Linux is Popular?</h3>

        <ul>
            <li>Free and Open Source</li>
            <li>Highly Secure Operating System</li>
            <li>Excellent Performance and Stability</li>
            <li>Supports Automation and Shell Scripting</li>
            <li>Widely Used in Cloud and DevOps</li>
            <li>Strong Community Support</li>
            <li>Efficient Resource Management</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Linux is Widely Used In:</h3>

        <ul>
            <li>DevOps</li>
            <li>Cloud Computing</li>
            <li>Networking</li>
            <li>Cybersecurity</li>
            <li>Servers</li>
            <li>Software Development</li>
            <li>Artificial Intelligence</li>
            <li>Machine Learning</li>
            <li>Embedded Systems</li>
            <li>Data Centers</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Popular Linux Distributions</h3>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Distribution</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Ubuntu</td>
                <td>User-friendly Linux distribution widely used by beginners and professionals</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>CentOS</td>
                <td>Enterprise-class Linux distribution used in servers</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)</td>
                <td>Commercial Linux distribution used in enterprise environments</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Debian</td>
                <td>Stable and secure Linux operating system</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Fedora</td>
                <td>Modern Linux distribution with latest technologies</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Kali Linux</td>
                <td>Specialized Linux distribution for cybersecurity and penetration testing</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <h3>Basic Features of Linux</h3>

        <ul>
            <li>Multi-user Operating System</li>
            <li>Multitasking Support</li>
            <li>Command Line Interface (CLI)</li>
            <li>Strong File Permission System</li>
            <li>Supports Networking</li>
            <li>Portable Across Different Hardware</li>
            <li>Supports Shell Scripting</li>
        </ul>

    </div>

    <!-- COMMAND SYNTAX -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>Linux Command Syntax</h2>

<pre>
command [options] [arguments]
</pre>

        <p>Example:</p>

<pre>
ls -l /home
</pre>

        <ul>
            <li><code>ls</code> → Command</li>
            <li><code>-l</code> → Option</li>
            <li><code>/home</code> → Argument</li>
        </ul>

    </div>

    <!-- NAVIGATION COMMANDS -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>1. Navigation Commands</h2>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Command</th>
                <th>Function</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>pwd</code></td>
                <td>Show present working directory</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ls</code></td>
                <td>List files and directories</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ls -l</code></td>
                <td>Detailed file information</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ls -a</code></td>
                <td>Show hidden files</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>cd directory</code></td>
                <td>Change directory</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>cd ..</code></td>
                <td>Move to previous directory</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>cd ~</code></td>
                <td>Go to home directory</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>tree</code></td>
                <td>Display folder structure</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

<pre>
pwd
ls -la
cd /var/log
</pre>

    </div>

    <!-- FILE COMMANDS -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>2. File and Directory Commands</h2>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Command</th>
                <th>Function</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>touch file.txt</code></td>
                <td>Create empty file</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>mkdir folder</code></td>
                <td>Create new directory</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>mkdir -p a/b/c</code></td>
                <td>Create nested directories</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>cp source destination</code></td>
                <td>Copy files</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>mv old new</code></td>
                <td>Move or rename files</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>rm file</code></td>
                <td>Remove file</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>rm -r folder</code></td>
                <td>Remove directory recursively</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>find</code></td>
                <td>Search files and directories</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

<pre>
touch notes.txt
mkdir projects
cp notes.txt backup.txt
mv backup.txt old.txt
</pre>

    </div>

    <!-- NETWORK COMMANDS -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>3. Networking Commands</h2>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Command</th>
                <th>Function</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ping</code></td>
                <td>Test network connectivity</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ip a</code></td>
                <td>Show IP addresses</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>curl</code></td>
                <td>Transfer data from URLs</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>wget</code></td>
                <td>Download files from internet</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ssh</code></td>
                <td>Remote server login</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>scp</code></td>
                <td>Securely copy files</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

<pre>
ping google.com
ip a
ssh user@server
</pre>

    </div>

    <!-- PROCESS COMMANDS -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>4. Process Management Commands</h2>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Command</th>
                <th>Function</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>ps</code></td>
                <td>Show running processes</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>top</code></td>
                <td>Real-time system monitoring</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>kill PID</code></td>
                <td>Stop process</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>jobs</code></td>
                <td>Show background jobs</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

<pre>
ps aux
kill 1234
top
</pre>

    </div>

    <!-- PIPE & REDIRECTION -->

    <div class="section">

        <h2>5. Pipe and Redirection Operators</h2>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Symbol</th>
                <th>Function</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>></code></td>
                <td>Redirect output</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>>></code></td>
                <td>Append output</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code>|</code></td>
                <td>Pipe output</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td><code><</code></td>
                <td>Redirect input</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

<pre>
ls > files.txt
cat log.txt | grep error
</pre>

    </div>

    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <div class="summary">

        <h2>Summary</h2>

        <p>
            Linux is one of the most important operating systems in modern IT infrastructure.
            Learning Linux commands helps in:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>System Administration</li>
            <li>DevOps Engineering</li>
            <li>Cloud Engineering</li>
            <li>Cybersecurity</li>
            <li>Networking</li>
            <li>Software Development</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Mastering Linux commands improves troubleshooting,
            automation, scripting, and server management skills.
        </p>

    </div>

</div>

